Zero OS in VirtualBox - x86/x64, BIOS and UEFI

Use this release's zero.iso. It contains both kernels and both UEFI loaders.
A 32-bit VM is now supported. BIOS selects 32-bit protected mode when long mode
or PAE is absent, and 64-bit long mode when both are present. Firmware boot
mode and CPU execution width are separate choices.

Recommended settings
Create an Other (32-bit) or Other (64-bit) VM. Use 128 MiB RAM, one CPU, PIIX3,
VBoxVGA with32MiB video memory and a PS/2 keyboard. For wired networking,
enable Adapter1 in NAT mode, select PCnet-FAST III (Am79C973), and mark
Cable Connected. Zero obtains its address automatically through DHCP.
The terminal is visible in the VM window; COM1 is optional. The color desktop
requires a firmware-provided 32-bit RGB/BGR linear framebuffer. BIOS attempts
VBE and otherwise keeps its VGA text terminal; UEFI uses compatible GOP modes.
The preferred graphic mode is1920x1080 with2x interface scaling. If firmware
does not advertise it, the loader tries smaller validated modes, preferring
800x600. A text fallback is an intentional compatibility path and does not
demonstrate color-graphics support.

Full HD firmware settings, with the VM powered off
Use the exact name of your VM instead of Zero OS. For BIOS, advertise a32-bit
Full HD VBE mode; for UEFI, request the firmware graphics resolution:
VBoxManage setextradata "Zero OS" "CustomVideoMode1" "1920x1080x32"
VBoxManage setextradata "Zero OS" "VBoxInternal2/EfiGraphicsResolution" "1920x1080"
These settings target different firmware paths and can coexist. They are
host VirtualBox settings, not commands in the Zero terminal. Host window
scaling or fullscreen alone does not change the guest framebuffer mode.
These configuration instructions have not been verified by a native
VirtualBox boot in this workspace; see the verification boundary below.

Add an IDE controller (PIIX4). Attach build/zero.iso as a read-only optical
disk at secondary master (port1/device0). Put Optical first in boot order.
Only the ISO is distributed; no writable disk image is supplied. For saved
files, create a new blank virtual hard disk in VirtualBox, for example64MiB,
and attach it as primary master (port0/device0), or attach your existing
ZeroFS disk there. Do not partition or format the new dedicated disk on the
host. Power off completely before changing firmware/CPU/storage settings.

At first boot, disks shows the new blank device as an unknown raw volume,
normally A:|. Select GO A:| and type format ZERO only for that new disk.
This erases and initializes its ZeroFS area. Existing ZeroFS disks need no
formatting. With no writable disk attached, the terminal/graphics still boot,
but file commands have no persistent storage.

BIOS: leave Enable EFI unchecked; the loader selects the supported kernel.
IA32 UEFI: use 32-bit EFI firmware; it opens EFI/BOOT/BOOTIA32.EFI.
AMD64 UEFI: use 64-bit EFI firmware; it opens EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI.
Secure Boot is not used. Firmware must match the selected CPU architecture.

Keep your chosen user-created disk across restarts and upgrades. Replacing
it with a new blank disk loses access to its files. BIOS32/64 and UEFI32/64 use
the same ZeroFS disk format; no reformat is needed when changing boot mode.
The ISO is read-only and cannot itself store saved files.
Additional user-owned compatible disks can use the other IDE positions. The
raw ZeroFS disk stays primary master. Volumes receive letters from A:;
use disks to inspect them and GO B:| to select another root. A physical disk
can contain several discovered partitions. Foreign filesystem support has
strict documented profiles; see DRIVES.ztext and fs/*-profile.txt.

Example: new 32-bit BIOS VM
Run from the extracted zero-foundation directory. Choose another name if
Zero OS already exists. Replace the absolute path with a new file location
for your own disk; the commands create that blank disk and the named VM:
VBoxManage createvm --name "Zero OS" --ostype Other --register
VBoxManage modifyvm "Zero OS" --memory 128 --cpus 1 --chipset piix3 --firmware bios --longmode off --pae off --boot1 dvd --boot2 none --boot3 none --boot4 none --graphicscontroller vboxvga --vram 32 --nic1 nat --nictype1 Am79C973 --cableconnected1 on --keyboard ps2 --mouse ps2
VBoxManage storagectl "Zero OS" --name IDE --add ide --controller PIIX4
VBoxManage createmedium disk --filename /absolute/path/zero-user-disk.vdi --size 64 --format VDI
VBoxManage storageattach "Zero OS" --storagectl IDE --port 0 --device 0 --type hdd --medium /absolute/path/zero-user-disk.vdi
VBoxManage storageattach "Zero OS" --storagectl IDE --port 1 --device 0 --type dvddrive --medium build/zero.iso
VBoxManage startvm "Zero OS" --type gui

The VDI in this example is created locally by your VirtualBox installation;
it is not a distributed image. If reusing an existing ZeroFS disk, omit
createmedium and use that disk's path. For a newly created blank disk, confirm
its identity with disks in Zero, GO A:|, then initialize with format ZERO.

To select the 64-bit BIOS path after powering off:
VBoxManage modifyvm "Zero OS" --ostype Other_64 --longmode on --pae on --firmware bios
To select 32-bit UEFI after powering off:
VBoxManage modifyvm "Zero OS" --ostype Other --longmode off --firmware efi32
To select 64-bit UEFI after powering off:
VBoxManage modifyvm "Zero OS" --ostype Other_64 --longmode on --pae on --firmware efi64

The --longmode/--pae aliases are accepted by the checked VBoxManage 7.2.22;
newer help also spells them --x86-long-mode/--x86-pae. Changing the OS-type
label alone may leave the explicit CPU-feature setting unchanged. The 32-bit
BIOS path does not require those features to be enabled.

Optional serial terminal
COM1 uses I/O3f8 and IRQ4. For a raw output log, with the VM powered off:
VBoxManage modifyvm "Zero OS" --uart1 0x3f8 4 --uart-mode1 file /absolute/path/zero-serial.log
For a Linux host pipe:
VBoxManage modifyvm "Zero OS" --uart1 0x3f8 4 --uart-mode1 server /tmp/zero-com1
After VM startup, a suitable host serial client can connect, for example:
socat -,rawer,escape=0x1d UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/zero-com1

Troubleshooting
"No bootable medium found": check that the current zero.iso is attached to
the optical drive and Optical is first in boot order.
The old "long mode unavailable" stop belongs to the earlier 64-bit-only BIOS
loader. Replace that old ISO with the current release; its dispatcher chooses
32-bit mode when the guest CPU lacks LM or PAE. info reports the selected
kernel width.
"Filesystem unavailable": attach your own writable virtual hard disk on IDE
primary master, not SATA, NVMe, USB or SCSI. Other supported volumes may use
other IDE positions. Use disks to inspect recognition. For a new blank
dedicated primary-master disk, GO A:| then format ZERO explicitly erases and
initializes it. Do not format an existing disk as a recovery step.

Verification boundary
In historical private tests, VBoxManage accepted the target configuration and
validated test VDI containers; those containers are not current deliverables.
The --longmode setting was explicitly checked in VM metadata, including an
OS-type label change that left the prior CPU setting unchanged.
Native VirtualBox VMs could not execute in this workspace: required host
virtualization devices/drivers are absent, the extracted hardened launcher
requires a system installation path, and private bind mounts are denied.
No native VirtualBox boot success is claimed.

The final 0.4.0 ISO also booted the 32-bit path under the exact VirtualBox
7.2.22 PC/VGA BIOS in QEMU with LM/PAE disabled, preserving a file after a
complete restart. Its VGA ROM is mapped at C0000. This is a firmware test
on QEMU hardware, not native VirtualBox.
The separate 0.6 VBE probe using VirtualBox's VGA ROM on QEMU's standard VGA
device did not advertise a usable VBE mode and retained text fallback.
That combination is not a native VirtualBox graphics test. Do not infer
native VirtualBox color-desktop success from either firmware result.
The VBox EFI firmware did not finish early initialization under QEMU and is
not counted as a successful UEFI test. UEFI32/64 development uses matching
OVMF firmware. See verification.ztext for the exact current release matrix,
artifacts, logs and remaining limits.

